Sliding Window Approach (Theory)

The Sliding Window technique is an algorithmic approach used to solve problems involving arrays or strings where we need to examine contiguous subarrays or substrings efficiently.
Instead of recalculating results for every possible subarray (which is slow), the sliding window reuses previous computations by moving a window over the data.

Why Sliding Window?

A brute-force approach often takes O(n²) time.
Sliding Window reduces this to O(n) by:

Maintaining a window range
Updating values when the window moves
Avoiding repeated work

Core Idea

A window is defined by two pointers:
Start (left)
End (right)
The window:

Expands by moving end
Shrinks by moving start
Slides forward one step at a time

Types of Sliding Window
1️⃣ Fixed Size Sliding Window

The window size remains constant.
When to use:
Maximum/minimum sum of subarray of size k
Average of subarrays
Fixed-length patterns

Steps:

Add the next element to the window
When window size reaches k, process result
Remove the element going out of the window

Slide forward

Example:

Find the maximum sum of subarray of size k

2️⃣ Variable Size Sliding Window

The window size changes dynamically.

When to use:

Longest/shortest subarray problems

Conditions like “at most k”, “at least k”

Substring problems with constraints

Steps:

Expand window until condition is met

Shrink window when condition is violated

Track best answer during the process

Example:

Longest substring with at most k distinct characters

General Pattern (Template)
start = 0
for end = 0 to n-1:
    add arr[end] to window

    while condition is violated:
        remove arr[start] from window
        start++

    update answer

Key Characteristics
Feature	Description
Time Complexity	O(n)
Space Complexity	O(1) or O(k)
Data Structures	Arrays, HashMap, HashSet
Applicable On	Arrays, Strings
Advantages

✔ Faster than brute force
✔ Clean and readable logic
✔ Ideal for interviews
✔ Scales well for large inputs

Common Sliding Window Problems
Maximum sum subarray of size k
Longest substring without repeating characters
Minimum window substring
Count occurrences of anagrams
Longest subarray with sum ≤ k